digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56921-8
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, understanding and predicting phase transformations is a crucial scientific task."];
  "N10" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, prior analyses mainly focus on initial hexagonal phases and provide only phenomenological descriptions of irradiation-induced phase behaviors."];
  "N11" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, there is no substantial theory explaining compositional trends in the dynamics of multi-stage irradiation-induced transformations in MAX phases."];
  "N12" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, there is a clear need for comprehensive understanding of multi-stage phase transformation behavior in MAX phases."];
  "N14" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, Cr2AlC is an anomalous case that does not follow the common hex→γ→fcc pathway observed in V2AlC and Nb2AlC."];
  "N15" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, Cr2AlC follows hex→γ→amorphous and does not form the fcc phase."];
  "N16" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, ab initio calculations can be used to explain compositional origins of phase transformation dynamics."];
  "N17" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, improved pathway energetics that include the intermediate γ-phase can capture γ-phase's key mediating role in irradiation response (unlike hex-only energetics)."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, irradiation is a common means to probe phase transformation dynamics because it can drive systems over barriers between stable and metastable phases."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) If a material shows multi-stage irradiation-induced transformations with an intermediate phase that mediates later outcomes, then analyzing only the initial phase will be insufficient; one should model/measure energetics across the full pathway including the intermediate phase."];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, for MAX phases, the γ-phase is an intermediate phase on the irradiation transformation pathway (hex→γ→...)."];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) If existing experiments/analyses cannot observe or explain the full multi-stage pathway and compositional trends, then a combined approach of in situ real-time observation plus ab initio pathway energetics should be proposed to obtain mechanisms and predictive rules."];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, for MAX phases, ex situ irradiation experiments cannot directly observe evolving atomic structure across damage accumulation, and initial-phase-focused analyses rarely study intermediate phases; therefore the complete multi-stage transformation pathway is not adequately explained."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the MAX-phase literature is insufficient for full-pathway/composition-resolved understanding and if such insufficiency holds then in situ+ab initio pathway modeling is needed, therefore propose in situ TEM irradiation plus ab initio pathway energetics to explain mechanisms and trends."];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, an anomalous pathway (Cr2AlC: hex→γ→amorphous) occurs while other compositions (V2AlC, Nb2AlC) follow hex→γ→fcc."];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) If different compositions show different multi-stage irradiation pathways, then a plausible explanation is that composition-dependent energetics (especially involving the intermediate γ-phase) control which terminal state (fcc vs amorphous) is reached."];
  "N3" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, many materials exhibit multi-stage phase transformations under irradiation (e.g., crystalline → disordered → amorphous)."];
  "N30" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given anomalous composition-dependent pathways and the knowledge that composition-dependent pathway energetics can select outcomes, therefore hypothesize that γ-phase-mediated energetics differ by composition and govern fcc vs amorphous outcomes."];
  "N31" [label="(0,0,0) If ab initio can quantify energetics along hex→γ and γ→(fcc/amorphous) and link to lattice/electronic descriptors, then one can derive a composition-based predictive rule using simple descriptors (atomic radius, electronegativity)."];
  "N5" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, MAX phases often follow a hex-phase → intermediate γ-phase → fcc-phase transformation sequence under irradiation."];
  "N6" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, most existing analyses for MAX irradiation transformations focus on the initial phase; intermediate phases are rarely studied."];
  "N7" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, it is challenging to understand transformation mechanisms if one analyzes only the initial phases."];
  "N8" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, using only initial-phase antisite defect formation energies cannot fully explain phase behavior nor yield generalizable prediction, because it applies only before γ-phase forms."];
  "N9" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, most MAX irradiation experiments are ex situ, so evolving atomic structure vs. damage cannot be directly observed, and the full multi-stage pathway cannot be described."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper aims to investigate irradiation-induced multi-stage phase transformations in MAX phases by combining in-situ TEM ion-irradiation of Cr2AlC, V2AlC, and Nb2AlC with ab-initio calculations of γ-phase energetics, and to test whether atomic radius and electronegativity can help predict extreme-environment phase transition pathways.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the retained and repaired conclusions identify the need to observe MAX-phase irradiation pathways in real time, model γ-mediated energetics across compositions, and test descriptor-based prediction of phase-transition outcomes."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Irradiation-driven phase transformations are scientifically useful targets for investigation because phase-transformation prediction is important and irradiation can drive systems across stable and metastable states."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Ab initio calculations together with γ-inclusive pathway energetics provide tools for explaining composition-dependent, γ-mediated phase-transformation dynamics."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The γ-inclusive ab initio approach motivates testing whether γ-mediated pathway energetics can be linked to composition descriptors such as atomic radius and electronegativity to formulate a MAX-phase predictive rule."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Given that existing MAX-phase experiments and analyses do not adequately observe or explain full multi-stage pathways and compositional dynamics, a combined approach using in situ real-time irradiation observation and ab initio pathway energetics should be pursued."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because MAX phases pass through an intermediate γ-phase during irradiation pathways, pathway-level analysis including the γ-phase is motivated to determine whether and how that intermediate affects later transformation outcomes."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) MAX phases can exhibit multi-stage irradiation-induced transformation behavior, including hexagonal-to-γ-to-fcc transformation sequences."];
  "N18" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, an atomic-scale mechanism can be developed by quantitatively describing energetics along the pathway and linking them to lattice/electronic characteristics of the γ-phase."];
  "N3" -> "N23" [label="induction-common"];
